  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n2 0 0\n0 2 0\n0 0 2\nOutput:\n2 0 0\n0 4 0\n0 0 2\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n0 0 9 0 0 0 0 0\n0 0 0 9 0 0 0 0\n0 0 0 0 9 0 0 0\n0 0 0 0 0 9 0 0\n9 0 0 0 0 0 9 0\n0 9 0 0 0 0 0 9\n0 0 9 0 0 0 0 0\n0 0 0 9 0 0 0 0\nOutput:\n0 0 9 0 0 0 0 0\n0 0 0 4 0 0 0 0\n0 0 0 0 9 0 0 0\n0 0 0 0 0 4 0 0\n9 0 0 0 0 0 9 0\n0 4 0 0 0 0 0 4\n0 0 9 0 0 0 0 0\n0 0 0 4 0 0 0 0\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n0 0 3 0 0 0\n0 0 0 3 0 0\n0 0 0 0 3 0\n3 0 0 0 0 3\n0 3 0 0 0 0\n0 0 3 0 0 0\nOutput:\n0 0 3 0 0 0\n0 0 0 4 0 0\n0 0 0 0 3 0\n3 0 0 0 0 4\n0 4 0 0 0 0\n0 0 3 0 0 0\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0 0 0 0\n6 0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0 0 0\n0 6 0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0 0\n0 0 6 0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0\n0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0\n0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0 6 0 0\n0 0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0 6 0\n0 0 0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0 6\n6 0 0 0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0\n0 6 0 0 0 0 0 0 6 0 0 0\n0 0 6 0 0 0 0 0 0 6 0 0\n0 0 0 6 0 0 0 0 0 0 6 0\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    result = [row[:] for row in grid]\n    # Find non-zero positions\n    positions = set()\n    val = 0\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                positions.add((r, c))\n                val = grid[r][c]\n    \n    # For each non-zero cell, check if it's part of a diagonal going down-right\n    # Replace with 4 if the previous diagonal step also had a value\n    for r, c in positions:\n        # Check if (r-1, (c-1)%cols) is also in positions\n        pr, pc = r - 1, (c - 1) % cols\n        if (pr, pc) in positions:\n            # Check if (r+1, (c+1)%cols) is also in positions \u2014 not needed based on pattern\n            # It seems: on a continuous diagonal, alternate: keep original at even steps, 4 at odd\n            # Let me trace the diagonal chain from its start\n            pass\n    \n    # Find diagonal chains: follow (r-1, c-1 mod cols) backwards to find start\n    visited = set()\n    for r, c in positions:\n        if (r, c) in visited:\n            continue\n        # Trace back to start of chain\n        cr, cc = r, c\n        while ((cr - 1) % rows, (cc - 1) % cols) in positions and ((cr - 1) % rows, (cc - 1) % cols) != (r, c):\n            cr, cc = (cr - 1) % rows, (cc - 1) % cols\n        # Now walk forward and mark alternating\n        start_r, start_c = cr, cc\n        step = 0\n        while True:\n            visited.add((cr, cc))\n            if step % 2 == 1:\n                result[cr][cc] = 4\n            cr, cc = (cr + 1) % rows, (cc + 1) % cols\n            step += 1\n            if (cr, cc) == (start_r, start_c):\n                break\n            if (cr, cc) not in positions:\n                break\n    \n    return result",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    result = [row[:] for row in grid]\n    # Find non-zero positions\n    positions = set()\n    val = 0\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                positions.add((r, c))\n                val = grid[r][c]\n    \n    # For each non-zero cell, check if it's part of a diagonal going down-right\n    # Replace with 4 if the previous diagonal step also had a value\n    for r, c in positions:\n        # Check if (r-1, (c-1)%cols) is also in positions\n        pr, pc = r - 1, (c - 1) % cols\n        if (pr, pc) in positions:\n            # Check if (r+1, (c+1)%cols) is also in positions \u2014 not needed based on pattern\n            # It seems: on a continuous diagonal, alternate: keep original at even steps, 4 at odd\n            # Let me trace the diagonal chain from its start\n            pass\n    \n    # Find diagonal chains: follow (r-1, c-1 mod cols) backwards to find start\n    visited = set()\n    for r, c in positions:\n        if (r, c) in visited:\n            continue\n        # Trace back to start of chain\n        cr, cc = r, c\n        while ((cr - 1) % rows, (cc - 1) % cols) in positions and ((cr - 1) % rows, (cc - 1) % cols) != (r, c):\n            cr, cc = (cr - 1) % rows, (cc - 1) % cols\n        # Now walk forward and mark alternating\n        start_r, start_c = cr, cc\n        step = 0\n        while True:\n            visited.add((cr, cc))\n            if step % 2 == 1:\n                result[cr][cc] = 4\n            cr, cc = (cr + 1) % rows, (cc + 1) % cols\n            step += 1\n            if (cr, cc) == (start_r, start_c):\n                break\n            if (cr, cc) not in positions:\n                break\n    \n    return result",
